WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:20.198
In the year 1866, something remarkable happened at sea, a mysterious and baffling phenomenon.

2
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:27.461
Although sailors were especially excited about it, the general public—even those who lived far inland—heard the rumors too.

3
00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:32.610
For some time vessels had been encountering "an enormous thing" at sea.

4
00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:35.838
This thing was a long, spindle-shaped object.

5
00:00:35.839 --> 00:00:41.761
People said it occasionally glowed, and it was larger and more rapid in its movements than any whale.

6
00:00:41.762 --> 00:00:48.610
On July 20 the steamship Governor Higginson met the thing five miles off the east coast of Australia.

7
00:00:48.611 --> 00:00:53.435
At first Captain Baker thought he was in the presence of an unknown sandbank.

8
00:00:53.436 --> 00:01:03.333
He was about to determine its exact position in the ocean when two columns of water shot a hundred and fifty feet into the air with a loud hissing noise.

9
00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:10.991
Three days later another ship spotted the thing—but this ship was fifteen hundred miles away in the Pacific!

10
00:01:10.992 --> 00:01:17.729
Fifteen days later and two thousand miles away, two more ships encountered the mysterious object.

11
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:26.658
Everyone began talking about the thing. They sang about it in the cafes, ridiculed it in the newspapers, and showcased it on the stage.

12
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:35.054
All kinds of stories circulated about the object, causing editors of scientific journals to quarrel with believers in the supernatural.

13
00:01:35.055 --> 00:01:43.483
The subject was debated for six months until a journal poked such fun at the topic that stories about the strange thing finally died.

14
00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:50.641
The topic seemed buried forever. But during the first months of 1867, new facts came to light.

15
00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:58.125
Suddenly the thing at sea was no longer a scientific problem to be solved but a real danger to be avoided.

16
00:01:58.126 --> 00:02:08.609
On March 5, 1867, a Canadian passenger ship, the Moravian, struck a rock that wasn't marked on any map for that part of the Atlantic Ocean.

17
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:17.530
Had it not been for the ship's superior strength, the Moravian would have gone down with all 237 passengers onboard.

18
00:02:17.531 --> 00:02:24.768
The accident happened just as day was breaking. The officers rushed to the rear of the ship and carefully studied the sea.

19
00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:31.093
But they saw nothing except the surface of the water violently swirling about 1800 feet away.

20
00:02:31.094 --> 00:02:37.927
The exact location of the place was noted, and the Moravian continued on its route without apparent damage.

21
00:02:37.928 --> 00:02:43.824
Had it struck a rock or an enormous shipwreck? The ship's officers had no idea.

22
00:02:43.825 --> 00:02:50.165
But when the ship's bottom was examined while undergoing repairs, workers found that part of the keel was broken.

23
00:02:50.166 --> 00:02:55.252
This incident might have been forgotten if the same thing hadn't happened a little more than a month later.

24
00:02:55.253 --> 00:03:04.831
Only in this case, the event caused a huge uproar because of the excellent reputation of the shipping line involved—the famous Cunard line of England.

25
00:03:04.832 --> 00:03:17.562
In 26 years Cunard ships had successfully made two thousand Atlantic crossings without a voyage canceled, a delay recorded, or a man, a ship, or even a letter lost.

26
00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:27.705
On April 13, 1867, with a smooth sea and a moderate breeze, the Cunard's Scotia was crossing the Atlantic, headed for England.

27
00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:34.197
At a little after four o'clock in the afternoon, while the passengers were having tea, a slight shock was felt.

28
00:03:34.198 --> 00:03:39.443
The Scotia had not struck anything; instead it had been struck.

29
00:03:39.444 --> 00:03:48.038
The shock had been so slight that no one was alarmed at first. But then a member of the crew started shouting, "We're sinking! We're sinking!"

30
00:03:48.039 --> 00:03:55.038
The passengers were very afraid, so Captain Anderson quickly reassured them that the ship was not in danger of sinking.

31
00:03:55.039 --> 00:04:02.006
The Scotia, divided into seven compartments by strong partitions, could withstand any leak.

32
00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:07.101
Captain Anderson soon found that the sea was rapidly pouring into the fifth compartment.

33
00:04:07.102 --> 00:04:13.219
He ordered the engines to be stopped at once, and one of the men dove into the water to determine the damage.

34
00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.927
He found a large hole, two yards in diameter.

35
00:04:16.928 --> 00:04:22.173
Such a leak could not be stopped, so the Scotia was forced to continue on its way.

36
00:04:22.174 --> 00:04:28.919
The ship was then about three hundred miles from Ireland, and after three days' delay, it finally reached a British port.

37
00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.411
When the Scotia was examined more carefully, the hole was found to be in the shape of a triangle.

38
00:04:34.412 --> 00:04:40.419
Something had punched its way through the ship's thick iron plates and then pulled out.

39
00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:44.983
From this moment on, the monster was blamed for any accident at sea.

40
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:54.229
During the time that these developments were taking place, I was returning from a scientific expedition to explore the Nebraska Territory in the United States.

41
00:04:54.230 --> 00:05:03.880
The French government had sent me, Pierre Aronnax, on the expedition because I am an assistant professor at the Museum of Natural History in Paris.

42
00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:11.809
After six months in Nebraska, I had arrived in New York City with a precious collection of minerals, plants, and animals.

43
00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:18.951
I was not due to return to France for a while, so I was busy organizing my specimens when the Scotia incident occurred.

44
00:05:18.952 --> 00:05:23.618
I was well aware of this unfortunate event and the debate over its cause.

45
00:05:23.619 --> 00:05:31.872
How could I not have been? I had read and reread every American and European newspaper without being able to form a conclusion.

46
00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:35.118
Something was out there; that much was certain.

47
00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:38.697
While I was in New York, the issue reached a boiling point.

48
00:05:38.698 --> 00:05:45.769
People had discussed a theory about a drifting island or reef, but that idea had been completely eliminated.

49
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:52.523
How could an island or reef move at such fantastic speed unless it had an engine in its belly?

50
00:05:52.524 --> 00:05:58.023
For the same reason, the idea of a massive, floating shipwreck had also been abandoned.

51
00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:04.840
There were only two possible explanations for the thing, and each explanation had its supporters.

52
00:06:04.841 --> 00:06:10.007
On one side were those who were sure it was a monster of colossal strength.

53
00:06:10.008 --> 00:06:17.078
On the other side were those who believed the thing was some type of underwater boat with tremendous engine power.

54
00:06:17.079 --> 00:06:24.586
But this second idea seemed nearly impossible. How could a private individual have a vessel like this at his command?

55
00:06:24.587 --> 00:06:29.166
Where had it been built, and how had its construction been kept secret?

56
00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:35.158
It was possible, however, that a nation might construct a submarine to use as a weapon of war.

57
00:06:35.159 --> 00:06:38.276
But rival countries are always watching each other.

58
00:06:38.277 --> 00:06:43.904
It was unlikely that one nation could have built a submarine without another nation knowing about it.

59
00:06:43.905 --> 00:06:52.055
Inquiries were made in England, France, Russia, Prussia, Spain, Italy, America, and even in Turkey.

60
00:06:52.056 --> 00:06:57.714
When the inquiries produced no facts, the hypothesis of a submarine was rejected.

61
00:06:57.715 --> 00:07:07.031
And so the idea of a monster surfaced again. Upon my arrival in New York, several people did me the honor of consulting me on the subject.

62
00:07:07.032 --> 00:07:13.198
In France I had published a two-volume work, Mysteries of the Great Underwater World.

63
00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:21.356
Well-regarded in academic circles, my book gave me a reputation as an expert in this obscure branch of natural history.

64
00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:29.580
By the time the New York Herald asked me to write an article on the subject, I had my own ideas about this monster.
